var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":1,"s":"Analyst Reza argues, with delivery figures attached, that the co-op should drop its overnight contract. Reza's spouse owns a warehouse that would gain clients if the contract ended. Citing only that fact, the board dismisses the figures and concludes the contract should be kept.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats Reza's spouse as an authority on whether overnight contracts should be kept","infers that the contract is sound merely because the contract and the co-op's profits once rose together in this instance","rejects the figures because of who would gain if the contract ended, rather than because of a defect in the figures","concludes that every co-op should keep overnight delivery from the case of this one co-op","assumes that a feature of each delivery is a feature of the contract as a whole"],"a":2,"e":"The only ground offered for setting the figures aside is the spouse's stake. The stake is asked to do the work of a rebuttal. The attractive choice — treats Reza's spouse as an authority on whether overnight contracts should be kept — loses because the board does not rely on the spouse's expertise. It treats the spouse's possible gain as a reason to ignore Reza's figures."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each page of the 1840 census volume is intact enough to be photographed flat. Therefore, the bound volume is intact enough to be photographed as one object on the archive's book cradle.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes a condition required for photographing a page to be enough to guarantee photographing the volume","infers that photography caused the census to survive merely because both are mentioned","treats the lack of a damage report as proof that no page is torn","infers that the bound volume has a feature merely because each page has that feature","takes for granted that the result could not occur at all unless the factor behind the claim that the bound volume is intact enough to be photographed as one object on the archive's book cradle was present"],"a":3,"e":"The credited description is the mistake this argument makes. The attractive choice — takes a condition required for photographing a page to be enough to guarantee photographing the volume — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Complaints were 30 percent of help-desk tickets last quarter and 12 percent this quarter. The manager concludes that the desk received fewer complaints this quarter.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that the share of complaints will keep falling next quarter, as the argument states it","treats a feature of each complaint as a feature of the whole desk","slides between two meanings of the word \"complaint\"","rejects the tally because of the manager's bonus","overlooks the possibility that total tickets grew enough to offset complaints' smaller share"],"a":4,"e":"The argument reads a change in share as a change in number without settling what happened to the totals. The attractive choice — assumes that the share of complaints will keep falling next quarter, as the argument states it — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who files a complete homestead claim receives a brass marker. Ellis has a brass marker. Therefore, Ellis filed a complete homestead claim.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the marker caused Ellis to file, because the marker and a filing are both in the argument","treats a condition sufficient for receiving a marker as though it were the only way to receive one","generalizes from Ellis to every homesteader in the territory","rejects the marker system because the clerk might profit from brass sales","treats two kinds of land claims as the only kinds that exist"],"a":1,"e":"Filing is enough for a marker. Having a marker is then treated as enough to show Ellis filed. That reverses a one-way rule. The attractive choice — infers that the marker caused Ellis to file, because the marker and a filing are both in the argument — loses because the argument runs from the marker back to the filing. It does not claim the marker caused the filing."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"Shoppers who redeemed a premium coupon were asked about the store's advice, and most rated it highly. The retailer concludes that most of its shoppers rate that advice highly.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["overlooks the possibility that the coupon caused the high ratings","treats a condition needed to redeem a coupon as though it guaranteed a high rating in this instance","lets the survey and the store's advertisements certify each other","infers that the whole chain has a feature shown only for one aisle","takes coupon redeemers to be representative of the store's shoppers on the question of advice"],"a":4,"e":"The people asked had already redeemed a premium coupon. Their praise is then assigned to shoppers as a group. The attractive choice — overlooks the possibility that the coupon caused the high ratings — loses because the conclusion is not that the coupon produced the ratings. It is that redeemers speak for shoppers generally. A causal aside is not the sampling step."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":1,"s":"The curator argues: either the sketches are locked in the vault overnight, or they will be stolen. They will not be locked in the vault. Therefore, they will be stolen.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the whole museum is unsafe because the sketch gallery is unsafe","dismisses a guard's report because the guard wants overtime","treats a night in the vault and theft as the only possibilities","treats last year's theft-free nights as proof about every future night","uses \"stolen\" to mean borrowed in the premise and taken permanently in the conclusion"],"a":2,"e":"Refusing the vault is treated as theft. That holds only if nothing else could keep the sketches safe. The attractive choice — infers that the whole museum is unsafe because the sketch gallery is unsafe — loses because the argument is about these sketches, not about the museum as a whole. The flaw is the missing third option, not a parts-to-whole leap."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"A nationally known cellist says the town's drinking wells should be dug twenty feet deeper. Therefore, the wells should be dug twenty feet deeper.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats a cellist's remark as sufficient to settle a question of well depth","rejects the water board's measurements because the board might gain from deeper wells","infers the depth from a correlation between concerts and water use","generalizes from one well to a conclusion that was already about the town's wells, which matches the cellist's own scope","slides between \"deeper\" as farther down and \"deeper\" as more expensive"],"a":0,"e":"Well depth is inferred from musical fame. Nothing shows the cellist applied a relevant standard to these wells. The attractive choice — rejects the water board's measurements because the board might gain from deeper wells — loses because no board is attacked, and no measurement is dismissed. The only premise is the cellist's remark."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No patrol note mentions a crack in the east seawall. The harbor master concludes that the east seawall has no crack.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that \"crack\" in a patrol note means a legal violation rather than a split in the wall","infers that every wall in the harbor is sound because the east wall is said to be sound","dismisses the patrol because the patrol captain rents boats","treats the absence of patrol notes as proof that no crack exists","projects last year's notes onto next year as the flaw, though the conclusion is about the wall now"],"a":3,"e":"Notes that never mention a crack are being asked to prove the wall is intact. That asks silence to be a search. The attractive choice — takes for granted that \"crack\" in a patrol note means a legal violation rather than a split in the wall — loses because the argument does not trade on two meanings. It treats missing notes as a structural finding."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Hasty generalization","d":1,"s":"The first three fossils catalogued from the north quarry were ammonites. The curator concludes that the quarry's fossils are ammonites.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["draws a conclusion about the quarry's fossils from the first three that were catalogued","treats two geological eras as the only eras that could be in the quarry in this instance","slides between \"ammonite\" as a fossil and \"ammonite\" as a living squid","infers that the cataloguing caused the fossils to be ammonites","rejects a rival curator because of a museum salary"],"a":0,"e":"The credited description is the mistake this argument makes. The attractive choice — treats two geological eras as the only eras that could be in the quarry in this instance — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The studio spotlight went dark during rehearsal. The stage manager concludes that the bulb has burned out.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the bulb has burned out follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","No cause other than a burned-out bulb accounts for the spotlight going dark.","The stage manager has replaced bulbs in this theater before.","The rehearsal continued after the spotlight went dark.","A spotlight with a new bulb cannot go dark."],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion needs there to be no rival account of this gap. It does not need the much stronger claim that the factor works in every case no matter what else differs. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the bulb has burned out follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The cedar footbridge has carried every harvest float for a decade. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that it will carry this year's float follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","The bridge will carry every float that is heavier than the floats already carried.","This year's float is not relevantly harder on the bridge than the floats already carried.","The parade has always used this bridge rather than the main road.","The float uses the same colors every year."],"a":2,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that it will carry this year's float follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pump in the plaza fountain is under the town's quiet limit. The engineer concludes that the fountain as a whole is under that limit.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","The fountain is under the quiet limit only if each pump is under the limit.","The quiet limit was adopted by the town council.","The fountain is no louder than its loudest pump, and no other part of it adds sound.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the fountain as a whole is under that limit follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"Engineer Okada argues that the elm footbridge should close and cites a crack measurement. Because Okada's cousin gains boat riders when the bridge closes, the council treats the measurement as void and concludes the bridge need not close.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that engineer Okada argues that the elm footbridge should close and cites a crack measurement follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Okada's cousin gains more riders whenever the bridge is closed.","Boat taxis in the area have lost riders this year.","Some residents prefer the view from the bridge.","A family stake in boat ridership is, by itself, a reason to treat Okada's measurement as void."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that engineer Okada argues that the elm footbridge should close and cites a crack measurement follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Offices that started a lunchtime walk reported fewer afternoon fatigue complaints than offices that did not. The director concludes that the walks reduced the complaints.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The walk offices and the other offices did not differ, aside from the walks, in a way that accounts for the fatigue gap.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the walks reduced the complaints follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Offices whose staff were already less tired were the ones that started the walks.","Some offices without walks also improved slightly.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the walks reduced the complaints, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion needs there to be no rival account of this gap. It does not need the much stronger claim that the factor works in every case no matter what else differs. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the walks reduced the complaints follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"This bus route has arrived on time every weekday this season. The dispatcher concludes that it will arrive on time next weekday.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that it will arrive on time next weekday follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Nothing about next weekday makes a delay relevantly more likely than on the weekdays already observed.","The route will be on time on every weekday next season as well.","Riders prefer buses that arrive on time.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that it will arrive on time next weekday, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that it will arrive on time next weekday follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A noted violinist, citing no well-depth rule, says the town well should be dug ten feet deeper. The council concludes from that remark alone that the well should be dug ten feet deeper.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that a noted violinist, citing no well-depth rule, says the town well should be dug ten feet deeper follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Wells in the next county are deeper than this well.","The violinist's judgment is a reliable guide to how deep this well should be dug.","The violinist has performed at the town festival.","Some residents own the violinist's recordings."],"a":2,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that a noted violinist, citing no well-depth rule, says the town well should be dug ten feet deeper follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Blocks that added evening streetlights had fewer collisions that season than blocks that did not. Therefore, the streetlights caused the drop in collisions.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the streetlights caused the drop in collisions describes, though not all of them do in this instance.","Some unlit blocks also had fewer collisions than they had the year before.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","If one set of blocks had extra streetlights and fewer collisions than another set, and no other relevant difference is given, then the streetlights caused the drop.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that the streetlights caused the drop in collisions."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the streetlights caused the drop in collisions describes, though not all of them do in this instance. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"No log reports a leak in the blue canoe. Therefore, the blue canoe has no leak.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the blue canoe has no leak describes, though not all of them do.","The blue canoe is rented more often than the red one.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met.","The premises were recorded accurately, which is all the argument has so far offered for the claim that the blue canoe has no leak.","Every leak in the blue canoe would have been reported in the log, and the log is complete."],"a":4,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the blue canoe has no leak describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"The trees will not be netted. Therefore, the crop will be ruined.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["If the trees are not netted, the crop will be ruined.","Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the crop will be ruined describes, though not all of them do.","More birds are nesting in the orchard this season than last season.","Some crops in the valley are netted.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that the crop will be ruined describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. The nursery's \"rich\" means that no fertilizer is needed. Therefore, this soil needs no fertilizer.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that this soil needs no fertilizer describes, though not all of them do.","The gardener uses \"rich\" in the nursery's sense.","Soil can look dark and still need fertilizer.","Some soils called rich are sandy.","The result comes about only if the stated condition is met."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Most cases that meet the stated condition turn out the way the claim that this soil needs no fertilizer describes, though not all of them do. — loses because \"most,\" or a condition running in the other direction, does not force this conclusion from these premises."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Members who switched to slow pottery wheels reported less wrist pain than members who kept the old wheels. The head concludes that the slow wheels reduced wrist pain.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the slow wheels reduced wrist pain.","Potters who already had comfortable wrists were more likely to switch to the slow wheels.","Before the switch, the two groups had similar pain and similar hours, and neither group changed its clay or its schedule.","A few potters on the old wheels also improved a little, though less than the switchers.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the slow wheels reduced wrist pain was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":2,"e":"A causal conclusion from a difference needs the sides to have been alike apart from that difference. The credited fact is the one that supplies that comparison. The attractive choice — The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the slow wheels reduced wrist pain. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Postcards fell from 20 percent of gift-shop items sold to 10 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer postcards were sold.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The later total was several times the earlier total, large enough that a smaller share need not mean a smaller count for the claim that fewer postcards were sold.","The later period included a holiday weekend and the earlier period did not.","Some visitors bought two postcards.","The shop sold the same number of items in both periods.","What the argument already reports about the claim that fewer postcards were sold was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":3,"e":"A smaller share is a smaller count when the total does not grow enough to offset the drop. The credited fact keeps the total from doing that. The attractive choice — The later total was several times the earlier total, large enough that a smaller share need not mean a smaller count for the claim that fewer postcards were sold. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. Using the nursery's sense of \"rich,\" a clerk concludes that the soil needs no fertilizer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The two uses of the key word do not share a sense in the argument for the claim that the soil needs no fertilizer.","In ordinary gardening talk, soil called rich can still need fertilizer.","By \"rich,\" the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the soil needs no fertilizer was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","By \"rich,\" the gardener meant soil that needs no added fertilizer."],"a":4,"e":"The step goes through only when both uses of the word mean the same thing. The attractive choice — The two uses of the key word do not share a sense in the argument for the claim that the soil needs no fertilizer. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"Either the trees are netted or the crop will be ruined. They will not be netted. Therefore, the crop will be ruined.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["This orchard will use no bird protection other than netting, and orchards that skip netting without another protection lose the crop.","A third course is already available and avoids the bad result named in the claim that the crop will be ruined, as the argument states it.","Birds are more numerous in the orchard this season than last season.","Netting is the most expensive option the orchard has priced.","Some buyers prefer fruit from netted trees."],"a":0,"e":"The bad result follows from refusing one option only when there is no third way. The attractive choice — A third course is already available and avoids the bad result named in the claim that the crop will be ruined, as the argument states it. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who finishes the dock course receives a green badge. Riley has a green badge. A clerk concludes that Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The condition treated as decisive is only one requirement, and it is not the only route to the result in the claim that Riley finished the course.","Green badges are issued only to people who finish the dock course, and Riley's badge is one of those badges.","Riley was seen carrying dock-course materials the week the badge was issued.","What the argument already reports about the claim that Riley finished the course was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","One side of the comparison in the claim that Riley finished the course paid more for equipment than the other side did."],"a":1,"e":"The inference needs the condition to be decisive in the direction the argument uses. The credited fact makes it so. The attractive choice — The condition treated as decisive is only one requirement, and it is not the only route to the result in the claim that Riley finished the course. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No tester found a crack in the blue canoe's hull. The outfitter concludes that the hull had no crack.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The search behind the claim that the hull had no crack looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the hull had no crack was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","The test used would have revealed any crack this hull could have, and the whole hull was tested.","One side of the comparison in the claim that the hull had no crack paid more for equipment than the other side did.","A case this argument never uses was settled by a different factor from the one in the claim that the hull had no crack."],"a":2,"e":"Silence supports absence only when an instance would have been recorded and the record was read. The attractive choice — The search behind the claim that the hull had no crack looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The regional water engineer who wrote the town's well manual says this well should be dug ten feet deeper, applying the manual to the latest measurements. The council concludes that it should be dug ten feet deeper.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The person cited for the claim that it should be dug ten feet deeper is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case.","What the argument already reports about the claim that it should be dug ten feet deeper was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","One side of the comparison in the claim that it should be dug ten feet deeper paid more for equipment than the other side did.","The manual's depth rules are the standard the council uses, and the measurements were taken correctly.","A case this argument never uses was settled by a different factor from the one in the claim that it should be dug ten feet deeper."],"a":3,"e":"A citation helps only when this source applied the relevant standard to this case. The attractive choice — The person cited for the claim that it should be dug ten feet deeper is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"Either the trees are netted or the birds will ruin the crop. They will not be netted. Therefore, the birds will ruin the crop.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["No third course is available, and skipping the protective option has already produced the bad result named in the claim that the birds will ruin the crop.","Netting is the most expensive protection on the price list.","Some buyers prefer fruit grown under netting.","The result in the claim that the birds will ruin the crop was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Orchards that skipped netting and used reflective tape kept their crops, and this orchard will use that tape."],"a":4,"e":"A third course avoids the bad result, so refusing the first option does not force it. The attractive choice — No third course is available, and skipping the protective option has already produced the bad result named in the claim that the birds will ruin the crop. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"A tablet ban reduced noise in the archive reading room. Therefore, a tablet ban will reduce noise in the clinic waiting room.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Archive noise was mostly tablet audio, while waiting-room noise is mostly door chimes and carts, and tablets there are already silent.","The new setting produces the result by the same mechanism as the earlier setting cited for the claim that a tablet ban will reduce noise in the clinic waiting room.","The archive has carpets and the waiting room has tile.","The clinic building is larger than the archive.","Some visitors dislike tablet bans."],"a":0,"e":"The new setting does not work the way the old one did, so the result does not carry across. The attractive choice — The new setting produces the result by the same mechanism as the earlier setting cited for the claim that a tablet ban will reduce noise in the clinic waiting room. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each of the four instrument cases is light enough for one person to carry on. Therefore, the full set is light enough for one person to carry on.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The whole adds nothing, beyond its most extreme part, to the feature at issue in the claim that the full set is light enough for one person to carry on.","Together the four cases weigh more than the streetcar allows one person to carry.","The four cases are the same color.","The result in the claim that the full set is light enough for one person to carry on was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","The person drawing the claim that the full set is light enough for one person to carry on expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":1,"e":"The whole goes past the limit that each part meets, so the feature does not carry from part to whole. The attractive choice — The whole adds nothing, beyond its most extreme part, to the feature at issue in the claim that the full set is light enough for one person to carry on. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":2,"s":"The bus has been on time every weekday this season. Therefore, it will be on time next weekday.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will be on time next weekday.","The result in the claim that it will be on time next weekday was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Next weekday the route adds a drawbridge that has been stopping traffic for twenty minutes at a time.","The person drawing the claim that it will be on time next weekday expected that result before the comparison was made.","A neighboring setting, which this argument does not use, showed a different pattern from the claim that it will be on time next weekday."],"a":2,"e":"The next case is relevantly different, so the past run does not reach it. The attractive choice — The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will be on time next weekday. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"Grit stopped puddles on the studio path. Therefore, grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The new setting produces the result by the same mechanism as the earlier setting cited for the claim that grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track.","The track is on a roof and the path is on the ground.","The result in the claim that grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Path puddles come from foot traffic packing the soil, while track puddles come from a roof deck with no drain, and grit does not add a drain.","The person drawing the claim that grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":3,"e":"The new setting does not work the way the old one did, so the result does not carry across. The attractive choice — The new setting produces the result by the same mechanism as the earlier setting cited for the claim that grit will stop puddles on the rooftop track. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent. The manager concludes that the desk received fewer complaints.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The total number of items was the same in both periods the claim that the desk received fewer complaints compares, so the smaller share is a smaller count.","Complaints are filed on the same form as other tickets.","Some tickets are compliments rather than complaints.","Ten percent is less than 30 percent.","The desk received five times as many tickets in the later period."],"a":4,"e":"A larger total can turn a smaller share into an equal or larger count, which undercuts a claim of fewer. The attractive choice — The total number of items was the same in both periods the claim that the desk received fewer complaints compares, so the smaller share is a smaller count. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"A tent on the green can host the festival without canceling it and without moving it into the civic hall. The director says the only ways to hold the festival are to cancel it or to move it into the civic hall.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The director's claim that those are the only two ways is false.","The director made no claim about the civic hall.","The civic hall cannot hold the festival.","The festival will be held in the tent.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a tent on the green can host the festival without canceling it requires."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The director made no claim about the civic hall. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef makes it. The only support offered for replacing the cables is a celebrated pastry chef's recommendation.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef requires.","The support offered does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","A recommendation by an engineer would also fail to establish that the cables should be replaced.","The cables should not be replaced.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef."],"a":1,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"A smaller share of a total establishes a smaller number only if the total did not grow enough to offset the smaller share. Bikes' share of the garage fell, and the garage now holds twice as many vehicles. Nothing else is said about the number of bikes.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The garage holds fewer bikes than it held before.","Bikes are now 25 percent of the vehicles.","The drop in bikes' share does not, by itself, establish that the garage holds fewer bikes.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a smaller share of a total establishes a smaller number only if the total did not grow enough to requires.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a smaller share of a total establishes a smaller number only if the total did not grow enough to."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — The garage holds fewer bikes than it held before. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation. Honey sales and a new weekend market rose in the same weeks. No other fact about a cause is given.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Honey sales rose for some reason other than the market.","The market did not cause the honey-sales rise.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation requires.","Those two rises do not, by themselves, establish that the market caused the honey-sales rise.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement is forced by the statements. The tempting one adds a cause, a reversed link, or a claim about other cases. The attractive choice — Honey sales rose for some reason other than the market. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"In three separate years, the harbor festival that hired extra streetlights had fewer evening collisions on the festival blocks than in the years it did not. No other difference among those years is described.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The streetlights were the only possible cause of the fewer collisions.","Collisions rose in every year the festival was unlit.","Streetlights have no bearing on collisions.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that in three separate years, the harbor festival that hired extra streetlights had fewer evening requires.","The extra streetlights are a serious candidate for explaining the fewer collisions in the years they were used."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The streetlights were the only possible cause of the fewer collisions. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent, and the desk received the same number of tickets in both periods.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The desk received fewer complaints in the later period.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent, requires.","Every ticket that was not a complaint was a compliment.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent,.","Most of a second group, not described here, also fall under the claim that complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent,."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that complaints fell from 30 percent of help-desk tickets to 10 percent, requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The engineer who wrote the town's well manual applied its depth rule to this well's latest measurements, which were taken correctly, and recommended digging ten feet deeper. The council treats that manual as its standard.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the engineer who wrote the town's well manual applied its depth rule to this well's latest requires.","The recommendation is on point for the depth decision the council is making.","Every recommendation by any engineer is correct.","Measurements never matter to well depth.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the engineer who wrote the town's well manual applied its depth rule to this well's latest."],"a":1,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the engineer who wrote the town's well manual applied its depth rule to this well's latest requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"It did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning, and an open irrigation line is the only other way that path floods. The valve's position was not written down.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning, requires.","Every garden path in the county was underwater.","The facts point more strongly to an open irrigation line than to rain.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning,.","Most of a second group, not described here, also fall under the claim that it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning,."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, the path was underwater this morning, requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener and the nursery both use \"rich\" to mean soil that needs no added fertilizer. The gardener called this soil rich.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The gardener meant only that the soil looks dark, as the argument states it.","The soil needs twice the usual fertilizer.","The nursery never uses the word \"rich.\"","On that shared meaning, the gardener's remark supports the claim that this soil needs no added fertilizer.","Dark soil always needs fertilizer."],"a":3,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — The gardener meant only that the soil looks dark, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule. Feldman applies that rule and concludes that the elm pilings should be replaced this year. The manual is the board's standard.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule.","Every piling in the harbor should be replaced.","The manual is the board's standard.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year and to treat every similar case the same way.","The elm pilings should be replaced this year."],"a":4,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — The elm pilings fail the manual's thickness rule. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"Each case is light enough to carry, but a feature of each case need not belong to the set of cases. So the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar.","The full set is too heavy to carry onto the streetcar.","Each case is light enough for one person to carry.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar and to treat every similar case the same way.","A further record of the claim that the quartet has not shown that one person can carry the full set onto the streetcar was kept by the same office across the period the argument discusses."],"a":0,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — The full set is too heavy to carry onto the streetcar. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"The path was underwater, it did not rain, the path drains within an hour, and an open irrigation line is the only other way that path floods. So the irrigation line was left open.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The path was underwater this morning.","The irrigation line was left open.","The path drains within an hour.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the irrigation line was left open and to treat every similar case the same way.","The evidence offered for the claim that the irrigation line was left open was gathered by the person who states it."],"a":1,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — The path was underwater this morning. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":1,"s":"The chef's remark is the only support offered for replacing the cables, and a chef's say-so does not establish a cable decision. So the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Cable decisions require engineering evidence, and the chef supplied that evidence.","The right response is to act at once on the claim that the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced and to treat every similar case the same way.","The chef's remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","The evidence offered for the claim that the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced was gathered by the person who states it.","The argument is mainly about how costly a mistake about the claim that the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced would be."],"a":2,"e":"The main conclusion is the claim the other statements are offered to support. The attractive choice — Cable decisions require engineering evidence, and the chef supplied that evidence. — loses because it is a premise, the denial of the conclusion, or a recommendation the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Honey sales and the new market rose in the same weeks. The clerk concludes that the market caused the sales rise.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that a word is used in one sense by the clerk and in another sense by the market vendors","attacking the clerk's motives for liking markets","citing a novelist as an authority on honey","breaking a percentage of sales into a count by fixing the total","inferring a cause from the fact that two things happened at the same time"],"a":4,"e":"The only link is that the rises coincided, and the conclusion is causal. That is the method. The attractive choice — showing that a word is used in one sense by the clerk and in another sense by the market vendors — loses because no word changes sense. The attractive description sounds technical and finds no second meaning on the page. The move is from joint timing to a cause."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The critic's \"original\" means a new design. The rule's \"original\" means the first physical panel. The councillor treats the critic's word as if it satisfied the rule.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["appealing to the critic as a legal authority on which murals the rule protects","inferring a cause from a correlation between reviews and demolitions","using one sense of \"original\" as though it were the other sense","generalizing from a matinee audience","concluding that a past record of protection will repeat"],"a":2,"e":"Two meanings of \"original\" are treated as one. That equivocation is the method. The attractive choice — appealing to the critic as a legal authority on which murals the rule protects — loses because the critic is not cited as a judge of the rule. The attractive choice sees a critic and invents an appeal to authority. The slip is between two meanings of one word."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats, so it will carry this year's float. This year's weight is not compared with the earlier weights.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["rejecting a sample of floats as too narrow to represent all parades","showing that two options for the parade are not exhaustive","defining a badge rule and then running it backward","projecting a past record forward without comparing the relevant conditions","lifting each pane and then concluding about a stack because the stack has one pane"],"a":3,"e":"A decade of success is the only ground for this year, and the loads are not compared. That is a past-to-future projection. The attractive choice — rejecting a sample of floats as too narrow to represent all parades — loses because there is no sample of spectators or parades being generalized. The attractive choice sees \"ten years\" and calls it a sample-size flaw. The method is a streak pushed one year ahead."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"**Honey sales rose in the same weeks as the new market.** The clerk concludes from that overlap that the market caused the rise.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the conclusion of the argument.","It is the only premise offered for the causal conclusion.","It is an objection the clerk rejects.","It is a principle that would license any causal claim in this instance.","It is evidence that the market did not cause the rise."],"a":1,"e":"The overlap is the sole support. The conclusion is the causal claim that follows it. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion of the argument. — loses because the overlap is not what the clerk is trying to establish. The causal claim comes after it. Calling the premise the conclusion is the standard role reversal."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"The rule protects the first physical panel. **The critic called the design new.** The councillor treats that remark as if it satisfied the rule.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise that uses a different sense from the rule the conclusion relies on.","It is an objection the critic raises against the rule.","It is the conclusion that the mural is protected.","It is evidence that no design can be new.","It is the main conclusion, and the rest of the argument is there to establish it in this instance."],"a":0,"e":"The bold statement does the job the credited choice names. The attractive choice — It is an objection the critic raises against the rule. — loses because it gives the bold statement a different job from the one it has. The usual trap is swapping premise and conclusion."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"**The table matches the harbormaster.** The clerk cites that match as the reason the table is reliable, and cites the table as the reason the harbormaster is reliable.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the conclusion that no further check is needed.","It is an independent calibration of both sources.","It is an objection to the harbormaster.","It is a premise in a circle of support.","It is the main conclusion, and the rest of the argument is there to establish it."],"a":3,"e":"The bold statement does the job the credited choice names. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion that no further check is needed. — loses because it gives the bold statement a different job from the one it has. The usual trap is swapping premise and conclusion."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":1,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats. **It will carry this year's float.** The marshal concludes that from the decade alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the conclusion.","It is the premise about the past decade.","It is an objection that this float is heavier.","It is a definition of \"harvest.\"","It is evidence that the bridge already failed."],"a":0,"e":"The marshal concludes the bold prediction. The ten years are the support. The attractive choice — It is the premise about the past decade. — loses because the prediction sits next to the decade and is easy to file as more of the record. The decade is the premise. The prediction is the conclusion the marshal draws."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if a clinic policy's only support is a trial whose volunteers do not match the patients the policy would cover, the policy is not established. The night-clinic policy rests only on a trial of retired volunteers, and it would cover night-shift workers who were not in the trial.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["Every clinic policy is not established.","The night-clinic policy is established.","Retired volunteers cannot give consent.","The night-clinic policy is not established.","Night-shift workers should be excluded from care."],"a":3,"e":"The only support mismatches the patients who would be covered. The principle says that policy is not established. It does not speak about consent or about every policy. The attractive choice — Every clinic policy is not established. — loses because the principle fires on a mismatched trial used as the only support. The attractive choice drops those conditions and condemns every policy. This policy meets the conditions. Others might not."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: if badges are lost for the same reason at two desks staffed in the same way, a clipboard that cut those losses at the first desk is a reason to expect a cut at the second. The craft museum and the science hall lose badges that way, and the clipboard cut the craft museum's losses.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The science hall will lose no badges.","There is a reason to expect the clipboard to cut that kind of loss at the science hall.","The clipboard result is irrelevant to the science hall, as the argument states it.","The halls must hire a guard for every desk.","The craft museum should abandon the clipboard."],"a":1,"e":"The desks match in the way the principle requires, and the clipboard cut the loss once. That is a reason to expect a cut, not a guarantee of none. The attractive choice — The science hall will lose no badges. — loses because the principle gives a reason to expect a cut, the same kind of result the craft museum got. Zero losses is a stronger promise than \"a reason to expect a cut.\""},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: a phone ban's success against phone noise is a reason to expect success in a second place only if noise there has the same source. Ward noise is mostly monitors, and phones there are already silent. The ban's only recorded success was against phone noise in a reading room.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The ward's noise is mostly phones.","Reading rooms should be closed.","The result in the claim that a phone ban's success against phone noise is a reason to expect success in a second place only if was already in place before the factor the argument relies on.","A further record of the claim that a phone ban's success against phone noise is a reason to expect success in a second place only if was kept by the same office across the period the argument discusses.","The reading-room success is not a reason to expect a quieter ward."],"a":4,"e":"Applied as written, the principle yields the credited judgment and not the tempting one. The attractive choice — The ward's noise is mostly phones. — loses because it is a judgment in the same setting, and the principle as stated does not yield it."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":1,"s":"Principle: joint occurrence does not, by itself, establish causation. The facts give only that honey sales and the market rose in the same weeks.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The market caused the sales rise.","The market sold no honey.","Those facts do not establish that the market caused the sales rise.","Sales rises are always caused by markets, as the argument states it.","The clerk weighed the honey."],"a":2,"e":"Only the joint rise is given, and the principle says that does not establish causation. The attractive choice — The market caused the sales rise. — loses because the principle says this pattern is not enough for causation. The attractive choice is the causation the principle refuses to grant."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The matinee poll is the reviewer's only evidence, and matinee audiences differ from the rest of the house. So the poll does not establish that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Any majority in any audience establishes the same majority for all audiences, as the argument states it.","A play is relaxing if a reviewer says so.","Matinees are the only shows that matter.","Polls are never evidence of anything.","A result about a narrower, differing group does not, by itself, establish the same result about the wider group."],"a":4,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Any majority in any audience establishes the same majority for all audiences, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The critic's \"original\" means a new design, and the rule's \"original\" means the first panel. So the critic's word does not show that the rule protects the mural.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["A conclusion that needs one sense of a word is not established by a premise that uses another sense.","Critics decide which murals a town rule protects, as the argument states it.","A new design is always the first physical panel.","Rules should be ignored when critics disagree.","All uses of a word mean the same thing."],"a":0,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Critics decide which murals a town rule protects, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The table and the harbormaster are each cited only for the other. So the table's reliability has not been established.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If the facts cited for the claim that the table's reliability has not been established merely occur together, that claim is established.","Two claims that rely only on each other do not establish either claim.","Agreement between two sources always establishes both, even if each is the only support for the other.","Circular support is the strongest kind of support.","The claim that the table's reliability has not been established is settled by the standing of the person who states it."],"a":1,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — If the facts cited for the claim that the table's reliability has not been established merely occur together, that claim is established. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":1,"s":"The only reason offered is a decade of lighter floats, and this float is heavier. So the decade does not establish that the bridge will carry it.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Ten years is too short to matter, so even a lighter float would prove nothing, as the argument states it.","Parade crowds have been larger in recent years than they were a decade ago.","A past success does not establish a future success when the relevant conditions have become harder.","Past success always establishes the next success.","Heavier floats are always safe."],"a":2,"e":"The credited principle is the rule the argument is using. The attractive choice — Ten years is too short to matter, so even a lighter float would prove nothing, as the argument states it. — loses because it is a nearby rule, often a stronger or reversed cousin of the rule the argument actually uses."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"Umbrella sales in Brine fell during the rainiest month of the year, even though rainier months usually bring higher umbrella sales.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that umbrella sales in Brine fell during the rainiest month of the year.","The rain fell mostly at night, when umbrella shops in Brine are closed anyway in every month.","Shops that stayed open stocked more umbrellas that month than in a typical rainy month.","A storm warning kept people home all that month, and the shops that sell umbrellas were among the places they did not visit.","Residents said in a survey that they like walking in heavy rain."],"a":3,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that umbrella sales in Brine fell during the rainiest month of the year. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The shop that cut its prices sold fewer units that week than the week before.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the shop that cut its prices sold fewer units that week than the week before.","The price cut applied to the shop's best-selling items, not only to slow movers.","The shop's advertising that week emphasized the lower prices.","Competing shops kept their prices unchanged that week.","The price cut was announced for the following week, and customers waited, so almost no one bought during the announcement week."],"a":4,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the shop that cut its prices sold fewer units that week than the week before. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The orchard that installed the recommended netting lost more fruit to birds than in the previous, unnetted year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A storm tore the netting down in the week the fruit ripened, which was the week the birds fed.","The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the orchard that installed the recommended netting lost more fruit to birds than in the previous.","The netting was the brand the extension service recommends for this fruit.","The orchard picked the fruit on the same schedule as in the unnetted year.","There were fewer birds in the orchard than in the previous year."],"a":0,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that the orchard that installed the recommended netting lost more fruit to birds than in the previous. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"Students given the longer study guide scored lower on the same quiz than students given the shorter guide.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that students given the longer study guide scored lower on the same quiz than students given the shorter.","The longer guide was handed out ten minutes before the quiz, and the shorter guide had been handed out a week before.","The longer guide covered every topic on the quiz, and the shorter guide skipped two topics.","The two groups had similar scores on a pretest the week before either guide was issued.","Students said they prefer longer guides when they have time to read them."],"a":1,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that students given the longer study guide scored lower on the same quiz than students given the shorter. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"After the honor code added more proctors, the school recorded more cheating findings, not fewer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that after the honor code added more proctors, the school recorded more cheating findings, not fewer.","The definition of cheating did not change when the proctors were added.","The new proctors graded scratch paper the old proctors had ignored, and that paper held cheating the old system missed.","Students were reminded of the honor code more often than before.","More proctors were added before the findings rose."],"a":2,"e":"The credited fact is the missing piece that lets both halves of the story be true together. The attractive choice — The step that was supposed to prevent the surprise was carried out in full, and people complied with it, during the same period as the claim that after the honor code added more proctors, the school recorded more cheating findings, not fewer. — loses because it confirms that the surprising half really happened, or it describes a different setting. It does not let both halves of this story stand together."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"The shop sold the same number of items, and postcards' share fell. Therefore, fewer postcards were sold.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The desk received the same number of tickets, and complaints' share fell. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Complaints' share fell, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Fewer complaints were received, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, complaints' share fell.","The desk received more tickets and complaints' share fell, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, fewer complaints were received.","Postcards are items, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, every item is a postcard."],"a":0,"e":"Both combine an unchanged total with a smaller share and conclude that the number fell. The share alone does not match. The attractive choice — Complaints' share fell, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, fewer complaints were received. — loses because the trap keeps the share drop and drops the fixed total. The stimulus uses both. A share drop alone is the flawed cousin of the valid argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"The chef's remark applies no cable standard, and it is the only support offered. Therefore, the remark does not establish that the cables should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["A violinist commented on the well, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the well should be dug deeper on those facts alone.","The violinist's remark applies no well-depth rule, and it is the only support offered for the digging. Therefore, the remark does not establish how deep the well should be dug.","The well manual's author applied the manual, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the remark is irrelevant to the well on those facts alone.","Chefs bake early, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, cables should be replaced on those facts alone.","Every non-engineer is wrong about wells. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind on those facts alone."],"a":1,"e":"Both say an out-of-field remark is the only support and conclude that the remark does not establish the decision. The attractive choice — A violinist commented on the well, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the well should be dug deeper on those facts alone. — loses because the trap treats the outsider's remark as establishing the decision. The stimulus says such a remark does not establish it. The well choice that withholds establishment matches. The digging choice does not."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":3,"s":"Each map folds into a pocket. Therefore, the packet folds into a pocket.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Each pane is light enough to lift, and the stack is a single pane. Therefore, the stack is light enough to lift.","The stack is light enough to lift, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, each pane is.","Each pane is light enough to lift. Therefore, the stack of panes is light enough to lift.","The packet is a map, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the map is a packet.","No one reported a heavy map, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, no map is heavy."],"a":2,"e":"Both give the whole a feature shown only for the parts, with no further premise. One pane would cure the flaw, so it is not the parallel. The attractive choice — Each pane is light enough to lift, and the stack is a single pane. Therefore, the stack is light enough to lift. — loses because the trap adds the single-pane premise that makes the inference safe. The stimulus and the stack-of-panes choice lack that premise and still conclude about the whole."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Most passengers on one midday crossing want a dawn trip. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want one.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want one would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether a few passengers on the crossing said no","Whether a written notice of the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want one was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether that midday crossing matches the ridership in trip purpose and how often they ride","Whether acting on the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want one would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong."],"a":3,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want one would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"No log mentions a jam. The director concludes that the motor has not jammed.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the motor has not jammed would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether a jammed motor is expensive to replace","Whether a written notice of the claim that the motor has not jammed was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that the motor has not jammed would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong.","Whether any jam would have been entered in a log that is complete for this period"],"a":4,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the motor has not jammed would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"The critic called the design \"original,\" and the rule protects an \"original\" panel. The councillor concludes that the mural is protected.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that the mural is protected would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether the heritage rule is more than a century old","Whether the councillor likes the color of the paint","Whether the critic's sense of \"original\" is the rule's sense","Whether a written notice of the claim that the mural is protected was posted where the audience could read it."],"a":3,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that the mural is protected would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. The board concludes that they should be replaced.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the person drawing the claim that they should be replaced would gain personally if that claim were accepted.","Whether a written notice of the claim that they should be replaced was posted where the audience could read it.","Whether acting on the claim that they should be replaced would be expensive if the claim turned out to be wrong.","Whether a similar claim was accepted last year in a setting this argument does not discuss.","Whether the chef is applying a relevant cable standard or speaking outside that field"],"a":4,"e":"The useful question is the one whose answers pull the conclusion in opposite directions. The credited question is aimed at the gap. The attractive choice — Whether the person drawing the claim that they should be replaced would gain personally if that claim were accepted. — loses because it can be answered either way without deciding whether the evidence reaches the conclusion."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Ade: The market caused the honey-sales rise, since the two happened in the same weeks. Bo: The two happened in the same weeks, but the clover bloom, not the market, caused the rise.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the market caused the honey-sales rise","the market both speakers mention was actually under discussion","honey sales and the market rose in the same weeks","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the market comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the market"],"a":0,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the market both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Greta: The garage holds fewer bikes, because bikes fell from 40 percent of vehicles to 25 percent. Hiro: The share fell, but the garage now holds twice the vehicles, so the share drop does not show there are fewer bikes.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the garage both speakers mention was actually under discussion","the drop in share shows that the garage holds fewer bikes","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the garage comes out","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the garage","the garage had never come up before these two speakers addressed it"],"a":1,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the garage both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Priya: This year's heavier float will be carried, because the bridge carried every lighter float for a decade. Quinn: The decade covered only lighter floats, so it does not show the bridge will carry this heavier one.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the year's both speakers mention was actually under discussion","this year's float is heavier than the earlier floats","the decade of lighter floats shows that the bridge will carry this heavier float","the bridge carried lighter floats for a decade","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the year's comes out"],"a":2,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the year's both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Vela: The lunchtime walks caused the fatigue drop at the offices that added them. Wes: Those offices also cut workloads in half, and the other offices did not, so the walks are not shown to be the cause.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the lunchtime both speakers mention was actually under discussion","the walk offices later had fewer fatigue complaints","one of the two speakers has a personal stake in how the question about the lunchtime comes out","the walks are shown to be the cause of the fatigue drop","every dispute of this kind has to be resolved the way the speakers resolve the lunchtime"],"a":3,"e":"The speakers are committed to opposite answers on the credited point, and not on the tempting one. The attractive choice — the lunchtime both speakers mention was actually under discussion — loses because both speakers can accept it, or neither has taken a stand on it. Disagreement needs opposite commitments."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Post hoc","d":2,"s":"The gallery hung a new curtain on Friday, and ticket sales fell on Saturday. No other change in price, weather, or programming that weekend is described, and the manager has not compared other weekends.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Ticket sales fall only after curtains are changed, as the argument states it.","The curtain must have caused the Saturday drop.","The gallery should take the curtain down.","The curtain had no effect on sales.","The curtain is a candidate explanation for the Saturday drop, not a cause the facts have isolated."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement stays inside what the information supports. The attractive choice — Ticket sales fall only after curtains are changed, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."}
]);
